# Security Rules — Aquarius DeFi Lab (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

## Security Philosophy
- Assume all inputs are malicious until proven otherwise.
- On-chain data is untrusted until validated.
- Internal services must not trust each other by default.
- Security > performance > convenience.

## Secrets & Credentials
- Never hardcode secrets, private keys, API keys, or RPC URLs.
- All secrets must be accessed via secure environment variables.
- Never log secrets or sensitive payloads.
- Mock secrets in development; never reuse production secrets.

## Authentication & Authorization
- Authentication does NOT imply authorization.
- Every sensitive operation must explicitly check permissions.
- Principle of least privilege applies to users, services, and APIs.
- Wallet address ≠ identity. Treat addresses as identifiers, not proof of trust.

## API Security
- Validate all request payloads using strict schemas (Zod).
- Enforce rate limiting on all public endpoints.
- Protect against replay attacks and request flooding.
- Never expose internal implementation details via APIs.

## On-Chain & Protocol Data
- Assume protocol data can be delayed, incomplete, or adversarial.
- Never trust event ordering blindly.
- Validate chain IDs, block numbers, and transaction hashes.
- Do not assume protocol invariants always hold.

## Realtime & Streaming Safety
- Apply backpressure to all streams.
- Never allow unbounded memory growth from subscriptions.
- Validate and sanitize all streamed data before emitting to clients.
- Protect streams from abuse and scraping.

## User Data & Privacy
- Collect the minimum data required.
- Never expose wallet-level earning strategies by default.
- Sensitive insights must require explicit user consent.
- Aggregate or anonymize data wherever possible.

## Logging & Observability
- Logs must never contain secrets or private user data.
- Redact sensitive fields by default.
- Treat logs as potentially public artifacts.

## Error Handling
- Fail safely and explicitly.
- Never leak stack traces or internal state to clients.
- Prefer graceful degradation over undefined behavior.

## Dependency & Supply Chain
- Avoid unvetted or abandoned dependencies.
- Pin dependency versions.
- Assume third-party services can fail or be compromised.

## Founder Rule
- If a feature weakens security assumptions, it does not ship.
- If uncertain, choose the safer option.


## Summary (Non-Negotiable)
- Never log private keys, secrets, or API tokens.
- Assume all external inputs are untrusted.
- Validate everything using Zod.
- Rate-limit all public APIs.
- Separate read-only endpoints from mutation endpoints.
- Treat predictive signals as advisory, not financial guarantees.
